      "text": "To find the greatest common divisor (GCD) of the function \\( f(x) = (3x + 4)(7x + 1)(13x + 6)(2x + 9) \\) and \\( x \\), we need to evaluate \\( f(x) \\mod x \\). Since \\( f(x) \\) is a polynomial in \\( x \\) and \\( \\gcd(x, f(x)) \\) gives the greatest common divisor of the expression, we need to find the GCD of \\( x \\) and each polynomial factor modulo \\( x \\).\n\nFirst, note that for any integer \\( x \\), \\( x \\) is coprime with itself. Thus, the GCD of \\( x \\) and each linear factor \\( (ax + b) \\) for any integer \\( a \\) and \\( b \\) is 1. This means \\( \\gcd(x, (ax + b)) = 1 \\) for any such \\( a \\) and \\( b \\).\n\nNow, we can evaluate the polynomial \\( f(x) \\mod x \\):\n\\[ f(x) = (3x + 4)(7x + 1)(13x + 6)(2x + 9) \\]\nWhen we evaluate at \\( x = 0 \\), we get:\n\\[ f(0) = (3 \\cdot 0 + 4)(7 \\cdot 0 + 1)(13 \\cdot 0 + 6)(2 \\cdot 0 + 9) = 4 \\cdot 1 \\cdot 6 \\cdot 9 = 216. \\]\nSince the GCD of \\( x \\) and \\( f(x) \\) is \\( \\gcd(x, 216) \\), we need to find the GCD of \\( x \\) and 216. Given that \\( x \\) is a multiple of 15336, we can write \\( x = 15336k \\) for some integer \\( k \\). Then:\n\\[ \\gcd(15336k, 216) = 216. \\]\nThis means that the greatest common divisor of \\( f(x) \\) and \\( x \\) is 216. Therefore, the answer is:\n\\[ \\boxed{216} \\]",
